//// jhonstart/client_runtime — the client build's hook runtime (decision 88 of
//// 1.0.10-beta).
////
//// `use f(x)` lowers to `f(x)` on every backend, so a component's `state(0)`
//// binds to whatever module `hooks` resolves to. On the server pass that is
//// `hooks.bp` — the pure first-render bodies. In the browser the client build
//// resolves `jhonstart/hooks` to `jhonstart/client_runtime.mjs`: the same five
//// nouns (`state`, `effect`, `memo`, `ref`, `reducer`), the same signatures and
//// shapes as `hooks.bp`, over jhonstart's own minimal render loop — cursor-
//// indexed cells, `set`/`dispatch` schedule a re-render, effects run after
//// commit when their `deps` change. The substitution is the client bundler's
//// step (front 68); under node it is a `require.cache` seed before the built
//// program loads — `examples/jhonstart-counter/client.mjs` runs `Counter` that
//// way. Outside a render every cell yields the first-render value, so the
//// module is a drop-in for `hooks` even when called plainly.
////
//// The five nouns are NOT re-declared here: a package's import surface is
//// flat, and a second `state` in the tree shadows `hooks.state` for every
//// `import { state } from "jhonstart"` (last module wins, silently — even from
//// a non-`pub` `mod`). The one cell below is the driver, and its
//// `./client_runtime.mjs` require is what makes the CLI ship the sidecar next
//// to every emitted module (G2). It is `#[@External.Node]` only and never
//// called from erlang, so the erlang emit carries it as an unresolved external
//// it never reaches.

import {element.Element};

// Runs `component` under the loop and hands every committed tree to `commit`;
// a `set`/`dispatch` re-runs the component and commits again. The handle the
// host returns (`cells`, `flush`, `dispose`) is the runtime's own — a driver's
// seam, read from JavaScript.
#[@External.Node("./client_runtime.mjs", "render")]
pub declare fn clientRender(
    component: fn() -> Element,
    commit: fn(tree: Element),
) -> i32;
